    "expert_task_2": [
      "WWR",
      "[Expert R1] (Detect atomicity violations in the code.)\n```json\n{\n    \"violations\": [\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"x\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Write\", \"Write\", \"Read\"],\n            \"lines\": [18, 11, 19]\n        }\n    ]\n}\n```\n\n[System] Expert first round completed in 13.39 seconds\n\n[Judge R1] (Validate the expert's defect report and check control/interrupt conditions.)\n### Read/Write Operation Accessibility Analysis\n\n**1. Write to `x` on line 18 in `svp_simple_601_001_main`**\n- **Control Conditions:** This operation (`x = 0;`) is not guarded by any control statements (`if`, `while`, etc.).\n- **Execution Path:** The `main` function begins execution. The code on line 16 (`assert(x == 1);`) executes, and since `x` is initialized to 1, execution proceeds. The write on line 18 is the next statement.\n- **Accessibility:** This operation is unconditionally executed as part of the normal program flow. It is **accessible**.\n\n**2. Write to `x` on line 11 in `svp_simple_601_001_isr_1`**\n- **Control Conditions:** This operation (`x = -1;`) is not inside any control block. The `if (x == 1)` statement on line 7 has an empty body and concludes on line 9. The write on line 11 is executed regardless of the outcome of the `if` condition.\n- **Execution Path:** This operation is inside an Interrupt Service Routine (ISR). Assuming interrupts are enabled (which is the default assumption in the absence of `disable_isr` calls), this ISR can preempt the `main` function. When the ISR is triggered, it will execute its code sequentially.\n- **Accessibility:** This operation is unconditionally executed every time `svp_simple_601_001_isr_1` is called. It is **accessible**.\n\n**3. Read of `x` on line 19 in `svp_simple_601_001_main`**\n- **Control Conditions:** This operation (`assert(x == 0);`) is not guarded by any control statements.\n- **Execution Path:** This operation follows the write on line 18 in the `main` function's execution flow.\n- **Accessibility:** This operation is unconditionally executed as part of the normal program flow. It is **accessible**.\n\n### Atomicity Violation Analysis\n\nThe reported atomicity violation describes the following sequence:\n1.  `main` executes the write `x = 0;` on line 18.\n2.  An interrupt occurs, preempting `main` before it can execute line 19.\n3.  `isr_1` executes. It checks `if (x == 1)` (which is false, as `x` is now 0) and then unconditionally executes the write `x = -1;` on line 11.\n4.  `isr_1` completes, and control returns to `main`.\n5.  `main` resumes and executes the read of `x` on line 19 in the `assert(x == 0);` statement. At this point, `x` holds the value -1 due to the interrupt, causing the assertion to fail.\n\nSince all involved read/write operations are accessible and the preemption scenario is valid, the atomicity of the write-read sequence on lines 18-19 in `main` is violated by the write in `isr_1`. The reported defect is a true positive.\n\n```json\n{\n    \"violations\": [\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"x\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Write\", \"Write\", \"Read\"],\n            \"lines\": [\"18\", \"11\", \"19\"]\n        }\n    ]\n}\n```\n\n[Expert R2] (Refine the report based on judge feedback, re-check for missed or false defects.)\nAbstain\n\n"
